why: ran because operand 4 is a command-substitution `$(...)` or runtime-dynamic value -- its value couldn't be resolved (first seen at 17:4 `foobar status --certs-current -- "$2"`); so dorc runs it, to stay safe (when unsure, run). to skip it, make the operand a literal Dorc can resolve+probe [resolve-dynamism]
why: site 1 guard [dorc-auto:foobar] -- licensed by the dorc-auto:foobar oracle's vouch (at foobar.oracle.sh:4) that it is already satisfied; the original bytes survive and the check re-runs live at apply (to stay safe)
why: site 3 guard [dorc-auto:foobar] -- licensed by the dorc-auto:foobar oracle's vouch (at book.sh:17) that it is already satisfied; the original bytes survive and the check re-runs live at apply (to stay safe)
hint: 'hork' (line 13) is unmodeled: it is the first wall -- an oracle vouching its convergence would elide it when converged, and un-wall 2 downstream sites
